{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-31646" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ is moving in the $x$-$y$ plane. Its position vector relative to a fixed origin $O$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨{t}^{3}-2{t}^{2}-t,\\,2{t}^{2}-4t+2⟩,\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the speed of the particle at time $t=2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062257/state.json" "file_sha256" "bce15726a33dd7f3d7ea8900aad3d3d0b93b6302083a79d244f86d536ed23153"} {"question" "q-31646" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we calculate the velocity vector $v.$ Differentiating the position vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-2{t}^{2}-t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2}-4t+2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3{t}^{2}-4t-1,4t-4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the velocity when $t=2,$ we substitute in the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(2) & =⟨3(2)^{2}-4(2)-1,4(2)-4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the speed at $t=2$ we calculate the magnitude of $v(2),$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|v(2)| & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062257/state.json" "file_sha256" "bce15726a33dd7f3d7ea8900aad3d3d0b93b6302083a79d244f86d536ed23153"} {"question" "q-31646" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062257/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e266a82853c33dde22dbd335a5596314f2d34e7ff913c9c0bcf44e27bf83b179"} {"question" "q-31646" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062257/state.json" "file_sha256" "bce15726a33dd7f3d7ea8900aad3d3d0b93b6302083a79d244f86d536ed23153"} {"question" "q-31646" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "12"] ["math" "5"] ["math" "7"] ["math" "\\sqrt{7}"] ["math" "⟨3,\\,4⟩"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062257/state.json" "file_sha256" "bce15726a33dd7f3d7ea8900aad3d3d0b93b6302083a79d244f86d536ed23153"} {"question" "q-31646" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "5"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062257/state.json" "file_sha256" "bce15726a33dd7f3d7ea8900aad3d3d0b93b6302083a79d244f86d536ed23153"} {"question" "q-121717" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The position vector $r$ of a particle $P$ (relative to a fixed origin $O$) at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨\\sqrt{4t+1},3t-2⟩,\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the velocity vector $v$ at time $t=2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062257/state.json" "file_sha256" "bce15726a33dd7f3d7ea8900aad3d3d0b93b6302083a79d244f86d536ed23153"} {"question" "q-121717" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The particle's velocity vector is given by the derivative of its position vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{4t+1}),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t-2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4t+1}},3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the velocity when $t=2,$ we substitute into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(2) & =⟨\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4(2)+1}},3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{9}},3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2}{3},3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062257/state.json" "file_sha256" "bce15726a33dd7f3d7ea8900aad3d3d0b93b6302083a79d244f86d536ed23153"} {"question" "q-121717" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062257/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e266a82853c33dde22dbd335a5596314f2d34e7ff913c9c0bcf44e27bf83b179"} {"question" "q-121717" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062257/state.json" "file_sha256" "bce15726a33dd7f3d7ea8900aad3d3d0b93b6302083a79d244f86d536ed23153"} {"question" "q-121717" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "v(2)=⟨1,3⟩"] ["math" "v(2)=⟨3,2⟩"] ["math" "v(2)=⟨\\frac{2}{3},2⟩"] ["math" "v(2)=⟨\\frac{2}{3},3⟩"] ["math" "v(2)=⟨\\frac{4}{3},3⟩"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062257/state.json" "file_sha256" "bce15726a33dd7f3d7ea8900aad3d3d0b93b6302083a79d244f86d536ed23153"} {"question" "q-121717" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "v(2)=⟨\\frac{2}{3},3⟩"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062257/state.json" "file_sha256" "bce15726a33dd7f3d7ea8900aad3d3d0b93b6302083a79d244f86d536ed23153"} {"question" "q-121730" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The position vector $r$ of a particle $P$ (relative to a fixed origin $O$) at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩,\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the speed of the particle at time $t=\\pi .$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062257/state.json" "file_sha256" "bce15726a33dd7f3d7ea8900aad3d3d0b93b6302083a79d244f86d536ed23153"} {"question" "q-121730" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we calculate the velocity vector $v.$ Differentiating the position vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the velocity when $t=\\pi ,$ we substitute in the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(\\pi ) & =⟨4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ,-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ⟩=⟨-4,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the speed at $t=\\pi $ we calculate the magnitude of $v(\\pi ),$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|v(\\pi )| & =\\sqrt{(-4)^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+0} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the speed when $t=\\pi $ is $4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062257/state.json" "file_sha256" "bce15726a33dd7f3d7ea8900aad3d3d0b93b6302083a79d244f86d536ed23153"} {"question" "q-121730" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062257/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e266a82853c33dde22dbd335a5596314f2d34e7ff913c9c0bcf44e27bf83b179"} {"question" "q-121730" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062257/state.json" "file_sha256" "bce15726a33dd7f3d7ea8900aad3d3d0b93b6302083a79d244f86d536ed23153"} {"question" "q-121730" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "5"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{7}{4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062257/state.json" "file_sha256" "bce15726a33dd7f3d7ea8900aad3d3d0b93b6302083a79d244f86d536ed23153"} {"question" "q-121730" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062257/state.json" "file_sha256" "bce15726a33dd7f3d7ea8900aad3d3d0b93b6302083a79d244f86d536ed23153"} {"question" "q-121739" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ moves in the $x$-$y$ plane (relative to a fixed origin $O$) with position vector\n\n$$\nr=⟨{t}^{2}-6t,(t-3)^{5}⟩,\\,t\\in [0,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nCalculate the times at which the particle is stationary." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062257/state.json" "file_sha256" "bce15726a33dd7f3d7ea8900aad3d3d0b93b6302083a79d244f86d536ed23153"} {"question" "q-121739" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we calculate the velocity vector $v.$ Differentiating the position vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-6t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}((t-3)^{5})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t-6,5(t-3)^{4}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2(t-3),5(t-3)^{4}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle is stationary when its velocity is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =0 \\\\ ⟨2(t-3),5(t-3)^{4}⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we need\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(t-3)=0\\, & ⇒\\,t=3, \\\\ 5(t-3)^{4}=0\\, & ⇒\\,t=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution $t=3$ is the only solution that satisfies both equations, so this is the only value of $t$ when the particle is stationary." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062257/state.json" "file_sha256" "bce15726a33dd7f3d7ea8900aad3d3d0b93b6302083a79d244f86d536ed23153"} {"question" "q-121739" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062257/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e266a82853c33dde22dbd335a5596314f2d34e7ff913c9c0bcf44e27bf83b179"} {"question" "q-121739" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062257/state.json" "file_sha256" "bce15726a33dd7f3d7ea8900aad3d3d0b93b6302083a79d244f86d536ed23153"} {"question" "q-121739" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "t=1"] ["math" "t=2"] ["math" "t=3"] ["math" "t=4"] ["text" "The particle is never stationary"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062257/state.json" "file_sha256" "bce15726a33dd7f3d7ea8900aad3d3d0b93b6302083a79d244f86d536ed23153"} {"question" "q-121739" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "t=3"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062257/state.json" "file_sha256" "bce15726a33dd7f3d7ea8900aad3d3d0b93b6302083a79d244f86d536ed23153"} {"question" "q-352611" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ moves in the $x$-$y$ plane (relative to a fixed origin $O$) with position vector\n\n$$\nr=⟨\\frac{3(t-2)^{2}}{t+1},\\frac{3(t-2)^{2}}{t+5}⟩,\\,t\\in [0,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nCalculate the times at which the particle is stationary.\n\nThe particle is stationary at $t=$  \n{{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062257/state.json" "file_sha256" "bce15726a33dd7f3d7ea8900aad3d3d0b93b6302083a79d244f86d536ed23153"} {"question" "q-352611" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we calculate the velocity vector $v.$ Differentiating the position vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{3(t-2)^{2}}{t+1}),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{3(t-2)^{2}}{t+5})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{3(t-2)(t+4)}{(t+1)^{2}},\\frac{3(t-2)(t+12)}{(t+5)^{2}}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle is stationary when its velocity is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =0 \\\\ ⟨\\frac{3(t-2)(t+4)}{(t+1)^{2}},\\frac{3(t-2)(t+12)}{(t+5)^{2}}⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we need\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3(t-2)(t+4)}{(t+1)^{2}}=0\\, & ⇒\\,3(t-2)(t+4)=0\\,⇒\\,t=2,-4, \\\\ \\frac{3(t-2)(t+12)}{(t+5)^{2}}=0\\, & ⇒\\,3(t-2)(t+12)=0\\,⇒\\,t=2,-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution $t=2$ is the only solution that satisfies both equations, so this is the only value of $t$ when the particle is stationary." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062257/state.json" "file_sha256" "bce15726a33dd7f3d7ea8900aad3d3d0b93b6302083a79d244f86d536ed23153"} {"question" "q-352611" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062257/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e266a82853c33dde22dbd335a5596314f2d34e7ff913c9c0bcf44e27bf83b179"} {"question" "q-352611" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jak